    "chunk_size_bytes": 1477,
    "chunk_text": "It is not just fiction. Officially there were more than 770,000 homeless people in the US in 2024 which represents an 18% increase compared to 2023. Paradoxically the per capita income in the US is about USD 360,000 per annum and there are about 1,000 billionaires; this number also represents an increase from previous years. This shows that the rich are getting richer while the poor are getting poorer in the richest country and this inequality has not been addressed for 250 years. According to neo-liberal economic theory poverty is a personal issue with no one to blame. If a person commits a crime for survival, e. g. theft, he is to be blamed.\n\nThough inflation is under control, the GDP is improving and\u00a0 macro-economics are stable, the real incomes, particularly of the fixed salary earners and daily wage earners in relation to cost of living have dwindled. The lower middle class is teetering on the brink of poverty. Many have fallen. Middle class has shrunk significantly. Though unemployment has improved, the benefits of employment are meagre and insufficient to meet the basic necessities. Even the employed may be forced to look for sources of additional income. When options are limited, they may be tempted to try their hand in crime accessible to them, like bribery, cybercrime, \u00a0prostitution, and even narcotics which are pervasive and readily available. There are rumours that university students are involved in drug trafficking and even prostitution."
